+# Mod-API (ACTIVE)
+
+Opened 2026-10-13, a player: the accessibility mod *"needs to be a module like a rust crate type of mod ... unchained from
+versioning ... it should work online"*.
+
+THE ANSWER, IN ONE PARAGRAPH. A mod for this game should not be a patch on the game's code, which changes a dozen times a
+day, and it should not need the game's source. It should be a **module**: a folder that names itself, names the one small
+interface it was written for, and names every event it listens to - as a crate names its edition and its dependencies - and
+the game should offer that interface and keep it. This page is that interface: `src/mods/` on the page, `app/lib/moduleFiles.cjs`
+in the desktop app, the Modules card in Settings > Mods & files, and the keys panel. Its first users, the three modules of
+**Beardd's Wayfarer** (a quest waypoint, pins, a guide trail), are NOT part of the game: they live, are tested and are released
+in their own repository (github.com/BearddOddity/beardds-wayfarer), so a module is updated by its author without a game release - the
+whole point of an interface. Not a DFU member: Ledger A row MODS1.
+
+## THE LAYOUT
+
+A module is one folder, `<user data>/Mods/<id>/`:
+
+```
+waypoint/
+  module.json     its manifest (required)
+  module.js       its entry: one ES module, `export default function setup(dfo) { ... }`
+  src/            (its own sources, if its author bundles them into module.js; not needed at run time)
+```
+
+`module.json`:
+
+| field | |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id` | a lowercase slug, `a-z 0-9 -`, up to 64; **the folder's name** |
+| `name` | what the player reads |
+| `version` | the module's own, dotted numbers, 1-4 parts - **never the game's** |
+| `api` | the interface version it was written for: a whole number from 1 |
+| `events` | every event it listens to (at least one) - its whole contact with the game |
+| `entry` | a `.js` file in its own folder, default `module.js` |
+| `author`, `description` | optional words |
+| `sha256` | optional: the entry's SHA-256, checked before it runs (a module's own build writes it) |
+
+Unknown fields are refused: a typo is not a silent default.
+
+## UNCHAINED FROM THE GAME'S VERSION
+
+`MOD_API_VERSION` is one whole number. The interface is **append-only**:
+
+- an event, once shipped, keeps its name and its argument list forever; a later version only adds events, and arguments at an
+  event's end;
+- so a module written for `api: N` runs on every game whose interface is `N` or more, untouched by the game's releases;
+- a module cannot name a game build (`gameVersion` and any other field is refused), cannot reach an event that arrived after
+  the `api` it declares, and is refused with its reason - not run broken - on a game older than it needs.
+
+The game pins every event at its seam: `test/mods1_api.test.js` reads the source for the call, and pins the whole event table
+name by name and argument by argument. A refactor that drops a hook is red in the change that made it.
+
+## THE EVENTS (interface 1)
+
+| event | arguments | announced by |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `questMark` | `questXZ \| null`, `playerXZ \| null`, `heading01`, `markKind` | `ui/enhancedHud.js`, every frame the HUD draws, in every scene: where the game's own compass marks the tracked quest, where the player stands and faces. `markKind` says what the mark is: `'quest'` for the game's own, the kind a module named with `retarget` (`'pin'`, `'destination'`, `'exit'`, `'waypoint'`; `'place'` unnamed), or null with no mark |
+| `quests` | `views` | `scenes/questBridge.js`, after every look the quest lens makes - `{ id, title, main, urgent, clockSeconds, words, latest: { lines }, target }` a quest |
+| `drawFx` | `renderer`, `world` | `scenes/hostMagic.js` `drawFx`, every frame the world pass draws its effects, under the renderer's own camera - the door a module draws through (`createParticleEffect`, `updateParticleEffect`, `drawWorldParticleEffects`); `world` is a frozen read-only probe of the scene's collider: `floor(x, y, z)` (floor height under x,z nearest below y, or null) `ray(origin, dir, max)` (distance to the first wall, or null), and `doors(x, z, range)` (the doors of the street near x,z as [x, y, z]) where the host has them, and `known()` (what the player's map knows of the way about the place: the cells walked, `"ix,y,iz"`, and each one-way step taken - a teleporter, a drop - as [from x, y, z, to x, y, z]; the dungeon's automap record, `automapWaySteps`; null elsewhere), and `waypoints()` (the region map's waypoints the player follows, `{ id, name, x, z }` each in this frame - asked afresh, since the open world's frame moves as it streams; null indoors and underground) |
+| `mapPick` | `sheet`, `dxMetres`, `dzMetres`, `quest`, `exit`, `dyMetres` | `ui/heldMap.js` `_destinationContext`: a right click on the local map (the street plan, or a dungeon's flat plan) that the sheet's own arm did not take - the place as metres east and north of the player (a quest's ringed building when `quest`; the dungeon's way out when `exit` - a click on the plan's exit mark, entranceHere, once found; `dyMetres` how far above the player's feet, where the map knows it - the dungeon map's own pin, `ui/automapSheet.js` notePinned, which may stand on another floor - else undefined), from the sheets' `destinationAt`; the sheet stands a flag there - shown while the compass marks the place a module took the pick as (`retarget`), hidden while another is followed, gone once that place is - and a right click on the flag sends both nulls. Nothing listens, nothing happens: the game takes no destination itself |
+
+A seam asks `modBus.has(event)` before it builds an event's arguments, so an event nobody listens to costs a lookup. A module
+that lists `quests` makes the quest look run even with the tracker, the herald and the marks all off (`modLook`), under the
+same baseline rule the faces keep.
+
+## THE HANDLE
+
+`setup(dfo)` is handed its own handle, exactly:
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| `dfo.api` | the interface version this game speaks |
+| `dfo.id` | the module's id |
+| `dfo.on(event, fn)` | listen; answers a function that stops listening. An event the manifest does not list is **refused** with a warning, not delivered |
+| `dfo.store.get(key, fallback)`, `.set(key, value)` | the module's own settings, apart from every other module's (`dfjs-module-<id>` in the game's own storage) |
+| `dfo.say(text)` | a line over the game |
+| `dfo.onKey('F7', fn, 'what it does')` / `'Shift+F7'` | an action on a key - the module's default; the player may give it another (the keys panel, below). Never from a text field, never with Ctrl, Alt or Meta held. Answers a function that says the key it has now, for a module that names its key to the player |
+| `dfo.retarget([x, z], 'exit')` / `dfo.retarget(null)` | ask the game to mark that place (`[x, z, y]` where the module knows its floor - handed on as `questMark`'s height, so the waypoint and the trail find its floor) on its compass instead of the tracked quest's - the HUD's own compass, and every module that follows `questMark`, see it (the last ask by an enabled module wins; a module switched off asks no more); `null` gives the compass back. The second argument names what the place is (a lowercase word), handed on as `questMark`'s `markKind` |
+| `dfo.t('module.pins.reached', 'Reached {what}', { what })` | words in the player's language: the game's own text core (`t`) reads its string table in the language the player chose, and the module's English where the table has no row yet, each `{name}` filled from the arguments. A module ships no translations: its keys (`module.<id>.<word>`, a dotted name the table can hold) join the game's catalog and are translated with the game's own words |
+| `dfo.markColour('#38d0e8')` / `dfo.markColour(null)` | colour the compass's own mark (`#rrggbb`) - the waypoint asks its own diamond's colour, so the two diamonds read as one; the last ask by an enabled module wins; `null`, or anything that is not a colour, gives the mark the game's own gold back |
+
+The handle is frozen and has no door to the net, the disk or another module. A listener that throws is isolated - the frame
+and every other module go on - and a module that fails 25 times is switched off for the session.
+
+## CLIENT-SIDE ONLY
+
+A module is code the player installed, run in the player's own client. It reads what the game already shows and draws on the
+player's own screen. The bus carries nothing to the server and changes no wire, `RELAY_VERSION` included; a session online with
+modules is, to everyone else in it, a session without. (`modApi.js` imports nothing of `net/`, and a test reads it to say so.)
+
+**A module is the player's own code with the page's own reach** - as trusted as anything the player chose to install. What the
+bus promises is the *shape* of a module's contact with the game, not a sandbox: it is why a module declares its events, why the
+host can check an entry's `sha256`, and why the shell reads only inside the Mods folder.
+
+## THE HOST
+
+`src/mods/moduleHost.js` `startModules`, run once at boot **off the entry's static graph** (BOOT2's ceiling) and only where a
+desktop shell offers a Mods folder (`daggerShell.modules`; a tab has none). For each folder, in order:
+
+1. read the manifest (`parseManifest`); refuse unknown fields;
+2. the folder's name must be the manifest's `id`; a second module with one id is refused;
+3. the interface it was written for must be one this game has (`moduleCompatible`);
+4. if the player has it off (`dfjs-modules-off`), it is **never imported** - none of its code runs;
+5. read the entry's source through the shell; check its `sha256` where the manifest names one;
+6. import it from a blob URL, require a default export, call `setup` with its handle.
+
+Whatever goes wrong with one is a line in the report (`running`, `off`, `refused`, `failed`, with the reason) and the rest carry
+on. `app/lib/moduleFiles.cjs` is the shell's whole contribution: it lists the folders named like modules and reads one entry's
+source - a plain slug and a plain `.js` name, nothing past them (no `..`, no separator, size-capped).
+
+## THE PLAYER'S SIDE
+
+Settings > Mods & files > **Modules** (`ui/modulesCard.js`): one row a module with name, version, author, what became of it at
+this start, and Turn on / Turn off. A running module is switched at once (the bus stops calling it); one that was off at the
+start was never imported, so its row says *On at the next start*. A tab, with no shell, says it needs the desktop app.
+
+Install: a module's folder is copied into `<user data>/Mods/` (Beardd's Wayfarer has an `Install.cmd` that does it).
+
+## THE FIRST MODULES (Beardd's Wayfarer, github.com/BearddOddity/beardds-wayfarer)
+
+Not in this repository: they are listed here because they are what the interface was cut to, and what its seams are tested
+against in spirit (each of their tests runs in their own repository, against a fake handle and probe).
+
+| module | listens to | does |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `waypoint` | `questMark`, `drawFx` | a diamond over the compass's mark (the compass's own diamond takes its colour) - coloured by its kind (the quest its look; a pin sky blue, a destination purple, the exit teal-green, Okabe-Ito colours a colour-blind player tells apart, each with its word before the distance) at its own spot on the floor, in perspective, with the distance; dim behind a wall; an edge arrow when it is off to a side (F7; Shift+F3 cycles its look: gold, ivory, cyan, magenta, large) |
+| `guide-trail` | `questMark`, `drawFx` | gold embers along the way to the compass's mark - the tracked quest, or a destination a module asked the compass for - planned round walls over the floor (F4 on/off, Shift+F4 its look): the embers are a pure function of a path and the clock; pools lie on the floor as translucent stains and sparks rise additively, so the trail reads on snow as well as in a dungeon |
+
+| `pins` | `mapPick`, `questMark`, `drawFx` | a right click on the local map sets the destination (any spot, or a quest's ringed building); F2 drops a pin where the crosshair meets the world, F3 cycles the compass quest -> the exit -> places -> quest, Shift+F2 removes the followed one; arriving removes it; a jump of 150 m (a new place, its own frame) clears them - but not the open world's tile step (819.2 m, the frame moving as it streams: the places move with it), nor a teleporter's jump (landing at the far end the map has just recorded, `world.known()`). The exit and a dungeon map's pin keep their floor and are reached on it alone. A waypoint followed on the region map (`world.waypoints()`) is led to outdoors: one followed anew takes the compass, the rest wait in F3's cycle. THE EXIT is off until the player pins it: a right click on the dungeon map's exit mark (`mapPick`'s `exit`), or near where they came into the place (kept silently, never shown; the loader's route, where the map cannot say), pins it and the compass, waypoint and trail lead out; it stays in F3's cycle, its flag clears it, a new place forgets it. No key of its own. It asks the game to mark the place (`retarget`), so the compass, waypoint and trail follow it with no module reading another |
+
+The guide trail plans round walls over the floor from the world probe (`route.js`), up hills and humps (a metre of rise to a metre of ground, no ledge), and - where that search cannot reach the mark, as on another floor - over the way the player's map knows (`world.known()`: the cells walked, by their stairs and slopes, and the teleporters and drops taken, each one way), to the mark or to the first teleporter on the way; where the mark is shut in a building and the host hands over `world.doors` it leads to the door nearest the mark (the street host does; the loader's released-client route has no door list and leads to the nearest cell). Shift+F4 cycles its look.
+
+Each is one folder and lives with or without the others; none reads another.
+
+### KEYS - and the game's own
+
+The game binds F5 (character sheet), F6 (inventory), F8 (screenshot), F9/F11 (quick save/load), F10 (HUD) and the letters, digits and arrows in play; in dialogs and the maps it binds F1-F8 again. The modules use F2, F3, F4, F7 (and Shift with F2, F3, F4, F7) - none a gameplay default - and each key does nothing unless the HUD has drawn in the last 400 ms, so a dialog, a menu or a map that uses the same F key is never also answered by a module. `test/mods1_api.test.js` reads the game's default bindings and fails if a module takes one, if two modules share a key, or if a key is not gated.
+
+**The keys are the player's.** Shift+F1 (no gameplay default of the game's) opens the keys panel (`src/mods/keysPanel.js`; also the Modules card's "Change module keys" button) - small, docked at the left edge so the middle of the view stays clear, the name its modules share said once and their actions grouped by module: every module action with the key it has now. Choose one (click, or Tab and Enter) and press the new key; Escape cancels, Backspace puts the module's own key back. A key another module action has is refused by name; Shift+F1 and the menus' keys (Escape, Tab, Enter, Backspace) are never given; a plain letter, digit, arrow or Space is allowed with a warning that the game may use it too. The choice is the bus's (`setKey`, kept under `dfjs-module-keys` in the game's own storage) and takes effect at once: the host hands every key press to `pressKey`, which answers it by the keys as they stand. While the panel is open it takes every press and frees the pointer. Its words come through the game's text core (`modBus.js` `moduleKeyWords`); the loader's host, with no text core, hands the same panel English.
+
+## KNOWN LIMITS
+
+- `drawFx` and `questMark` are announced by the world-hosted scenes (the street, a building, a dungeon entered from the world).
+  The standalone dungeon page (`?shot` probes) does not announce `drawFx`: it is a test host, not one a player boots.
+- A module sees the compass's mark, which is the quest's place (the tracked quest, a place the player's map holds) and, inside a
+  dungeon or building, the Exact Quest-guidance tier's marks; it is never told a place the game would not already show.
+- The guide trail plans over the world probe (`floor`, `ray`, `doors`, `known`): it lies on the floor and the terrain and goes
+  round walls, but it only knows the way between a dungeon's floors that the player's map has recorded, and where the mark is
+  shut inside a building it leads to the nearest door (the street's door list), not through it.
+- Pins and destinations last for the scene: a door, a dungeon or a travel clears them (each place has its own frame).
+- A module's words read in the player's language only once the game's catalog holds their rows: `tools/l10nExtract.mjs` reads
+  `t` calls in `src/` today, so the modules' `dfo.t` keys are English until the catalog takes them in (and the pipeline drafts
+  them like every other row).
+
+## PINNED
+
+`test/mods1_api.test.js`: the manifest (every refusal), the version law, the event table, the bus (isolation, failure limit,
+enabled/disabled, a module's own store, the handle's exact shape, nothing of the net), the host (running, off, refused, failed,
+hash mismatch, wrong folder, duplicate, no shell), the player's switches, the shell's door (a real temp folder: slugs, plain
+names, nothing past them), the seams by source, and the Modules card over a fake pane. Mutants: the bus's listing law, its
+disabled skip, its once-only warning, the version check, the unknown-field refusal, the host's off skip, its hash check, its
+folder check, the shell's name check, the event table's argument order.
