e step further along: a probe whose EXIT CODE has nothing to do with whether its subject worked. T2 caught `guildServiceProbe` printing OK and exiting 0 on a run where its character had been turned away at the Mages Guild door and no service ran at all. Rule: every browser probe must judge something, and must not print three or more states AFTER its last judgement - that second clause is what catches the guildService shape, whose three exits were all setup guards with ten unjudged prints behind them. EYEBALL TOOLS are exempt (screenshot.mjs, musicRender.mjs and four others whose output is a PNG or a WAV for a human), and that allowlist is self-checking too: a member must actually write a file. THE SURVEY THAT FOUND THE SIX WAS WRONG THREE TIMES FIRST, always accusing working code by missing an idiom - the local `die()`/`fail()` helpers, then the `check()` harness, then the accumulate-and-exit shape `process.exit(ok ? 0 : 1)` that castProbe and firstHourProbe use between them for 45 judgements. The vocabulary is written down in the file so the next sweep does not re-derive it wrong. ROAD-E E8 added three, one per probe of Port-Ledger.md:836: the retired `shopProbe.mjs` name must stay absent while `tradeModeProbe`/`nativeTradeProbe` carry its subject; `toneProbe.mjs` may not hunt the `tone: Normal` TEXT row the native talk window draws as art, and must read `native` and `topicMode` instead; and `worldWhereIsProbe.mjs`'s boot-box drain must sit BEFORE its walker poll and judge itself - a modal holds the motor, so a probe that polls first polls a frozen world. |
