y the slice that quoted the warning. MW-D5's tests could not see it because every fixture spoke SkinRoot/Bone0/Bone1 and named its shapes "Skinned"/"PartSkin", so assembly never ran with two bones at all. MW-D6 authored four fixtures in MORROWIND'S OWN VOCABULARY through the same independent pyffi writer (armskel a two-sided CHAIN so a parent-chain mutant cannot survive; armhand carrying BOTH sides as `Tri Left Hand`/`Tri Right Hand`; armcuff rigid and ASYMMETRIC in x so the mirror is measurable; armnameless for the port's own nameless-shape extension) - which CLOSES the two gaps MW-D5 recorded as unreachable: rule 15's ACCEPT path and rule 13's mirror DERIVATION (`/left/i` on the bone name, which the direct placeAtBone call could never test because it takes `mirror` as an argument) now run END TO END with no `bones` override. Pins: both rules end-to-end, the nameless latch binding once, a refused part becoming a NOTE without abandoning the arm, and armPieceRows keyed by slot AND bone with PER-PIECE bounds (slot alone collides on every arm slot). 14 mutants, 14 dead - including three MW-D5 had recorded as unkillable. The generator was re-run first and reproduced every committed fixture BYTE-FOR-BYTE, which is what makes new fixtures from it trustworthy |
